<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alberta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alberta is a province of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>, with Edmonton as its capital and Calgary as its largest city. Once part of Rupert's Land and the North-Western Territory, it became a province in 1905. Its economy rests on farming, ranching and, since the discovery at Leduc in 1947, oil and gas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alberta.jpg" alt="Alberta" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kooma (original) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Alberta.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Alberta is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a> to the east, the Northwest Territories to the north and the U.S. state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> to the south. Its southern border is the 49th parallel north and its northern border the 60th parallel, and its western boundary follows the Continental Divide in the Rocky Mountains. The eastern part of the province lies on the Great Plains, and the Rocky Mountains cast a rain shadow over much of it.</p><p>The province was named after Princess Louise Caroline Alberta (1848–1939), the fourth daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and wife of John Campbell, Marquess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorn">Lorne</a>, Governor General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> (1878–83). Alberta has a rich diversity of Late Cretaceous dinosaur fossils, with at least 38 dinosaur type specimens collected there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleo-indians">Paleo-Indians</a> arrived in what is now Alberta at least 10,000 years ago, toward the end of the last ice age. Over time they differentiated into various First Nations. The Plains peoples of the south, such as those of the Blackfoot Confederacy and the Plains Cree, generally lived by hunting buffalo, while northerly peoples such as the Woodland Cree and Chipewyan hunted and trapped other animals and fished. The territory was also used by fur traders of the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-company">North West Company</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> bought the lands that would become Alberta in 1870. From the late 1800s to the early 1900s many immigrants arrived, in an effort to prevent the prairies from being annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and growing wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranching became very profitable. The Alberta Act of 1905 created the province.</p><p>Until the 1930s politics was shared between the Liberals and the agrarian United Farmers of Alberta, and the Social Credit Party then held office continually from 1935 to 1971. Massive oil reserves were discovered in 1947, and the exploitation of the oil sands began in 1967. In 1950 Alberta became the second province, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>, to adopt a publicly funded health programme, a precursor of the modern medicare system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Alberta is a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> with Edmonton as its capital and Calgary as its largest city, and English is its official language. It is governed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a> with a unicameral Legislative Assembly. Six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> lie within it: the Canadian Rocky Mountain Parks, Dinosaur Provincial Park, Head-Smashed-In Buffalo Jump, Waterton-Glacier International Peace Park, Wood Buffalo National Park and Writing-on-Stone Provincial Park.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alberta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Alberta</b> and Saskatchewan became provinces in 1905, and Newfoundland joined after referendums in 1949.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Today they live on the Siksika, Kainai and Piikani reserves in <b>Alberta</b> and the Blackfeet Reservation in Montana.</blockquote><a href="/state/blackfoot-confederacy">Blackfoot Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The grant covered much of what is now northern Quebec and Ontario, Manitoba, Saskatchewan, <b>Alberta</b> and parts of the northern United States, though the company knew little of the interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/ruperts-land">Rupert's Land</a></li></ul></section>
